  277|  5.95k|static int secp256k1_ecmult_const_xonly(secp256k1_fe* r, const secp256k1_fe *n, const secp256k1_fe *d, const secp256k1_scalar *q, int known_on_curve) {
  278|       |
  279|       |    /* This algorithm is a generalization of Peter Dettman's technique for
  280|       |     * avoiding the square root in a random-basepoint x-only multiplication
  281|       |     * on a Weierstrass curve:
  282|       |     * https://mailarchive.ietf.org/arch/msg/cfrg/7DyYY6gg32wDgHAhgSb6XxMDlJA/
  283|       |     *
  284|       |     *
  285|       |     * === Background: the effective affine technique ===
  286|       |     *
  287|       |     * Let phi_u be the isomorphism that maps (x, y) on secp256k1 curve y^2 = x^3 + 7 to
  288|       |     * x' = u^2*x, y' = u^3*y on curve y'^2 = x'^3 + u^6*7. This new curve has the same order as
  289|       |     * the original (it is isomorphic), but moreover, has the same addition/doubling formulas, as
  290|       |     * the curve b=7 coefficient does not appear in those formulas (or at least does not appear in
  291|       |     * the formulas implemented in this codebase, both affine and Jacobian). See also Example 9.5.2
  292|       |     * in https://www.math.auckland.ac.nz/~sgal018/crypto-book/ch9.pdf.
  293|       |     *
  294|       |     * This means any linear combination of secp256k1 points can be computed by applying phi_u
  295|       |     * (with non-zero u) on all input points (including the generator, if used), computing the
  296|       |     * linear combination on the isomorphic curve (using the same group laws), and then applying
  297|       |     * phi_u^{-1} to get back to secp256k1.
  298|       |     *
  299|       |     * Switching to Jacobian coordinates, note that phi_u applied to (X, Y, Z) is simply
  300|       |     * (X, Y, Z/u). Thus, if we want to compute (X1, Y1, Z) + (X2, Y2, Z), with identical Z
  301|       |     * coordinates, we can use phi_Z to transform it to (X1, Y1, 1) + (X2, Y2, 1) on an isomorphic
  302|       |     * curve where the affine addition formula can be used instead.
  303|       |     * If (X3, Y3, Z3) = (X1, Y1) + (X2, Y2) on that curve, then our answer on secp256k1 is
  304|       |     * (X3, Y3, Z3*Z).
  305|       |     *
  306|       |     * This is the effective affine technique: if we have a linear combination of group elements
  307|       |     * to compute, and all those group elements have the same Z coordinate, we can simply pretend
  308|       |     * that all those Z coordinates are 1, perform the computation that way, and then multiply the
  309|       |     * original Z coordinate back in.
  310|       |     *
  311|       |     * The technique works on any a=0 short Weierstrass curve. It is possible to generalize it to
  312|       |     * other curves too, but there the isomorphic curves will have different 'a' coefficients,
  313|       |     * which typically does affect the group laws.
  314|       |     *
  315|       |     *
  316|       |     * === Avoiding the square root for x-only point multiplication ===
  317|       |     *
  318|       |     * In this function, we want to compute the X coordinate of q*(n/d, y), for
  319|       |     * y = sqrt((n/d)^3 + 7). Its negation would also be a valid Y coordinate, but by convention
  320|       |     * we pick whatever sqrt returns (which we assume to be a deterministic function).
  321|       |     *
  322|       |     * Let g = y^2*d^3 = n^3 + 7*d^3. This also means y = sqrt(g/d^3).
  323|       |     * Further let v = sqrt(d*g), which must exist as d*g = y^2*d^4 = (y*d^2)^2.
  324|       |     *
  325|       |     * The input point (n/d, y) also has Jacobian coordinates:
  326|       |     *
  327|       |     *     (n/d, y, 1)
  328|       |     *   = (n/d * v^2, y * v^3, v)
  329|       |     *   = (n/d * d*g, y * sqrt(d^3*g^3), v)
  330|       |     *   = (n/d * d*g, sqrt(y^2 * d^3*g^3), v)
  331|       |     *   = (n*g, sqrt(g/d^3 * d^3*g^3), v)
  332|       |     *   = (n*g, sqrt(g^4), v)
  333|       |     *   = (n*g, g^2, v)
  334|       |     *
  335|       |     * It is easy to verify that both (n*g, g^2, v) and its negation (n*g, -g^2, v) have affine X
  336|       |     * coordinate n/d, and this holds even when the square root function doesn't have a
  337|       |     * deterministic sign. We choose the (n*g, g^2, v) version.
  338|       |     *
  339|       |     * Now switch to the effective affine curve using phi_v, where the input point has coordinates
  340|       |     * (n*g, g^2). Compute (X, Y, Z) = q * (n*g, g^2) there.
  341|       |     *
  342|       |     * Back on secp256k1, that means q * (n*g, g^2, v) = (X, Y, v*Z). This last point has affine X
  343|       |     * coordinate X / (v^2*Z^2) = X / (d*g*Z^2). Determining the affine Y coordinate would involve
  344|       |     * a square root, but as long as we only care about the resulting X coordinate, no square root
  345|       |     * is needed anywhere in this computation.
  346|       |     */
